822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (The Polling Booth System developed using HTML, CSS, JavaScript, and Local Storage successfully demonstrates the practical implementation of a web-based digital voting application. The system effectively replaces traditional manual voting methods by offering a faster, more reliable, and user-friendly alternative. By eliminating the need for paper ballots, manual vote counting, and physical polling infrastructure, the proposed system significantly reduces operational cost, time consumption, and human effort. The use of front-end technologies ensures smooth interaction between the user and the system, while Local Storage provides an efficient way to store and manage voting data without requiring an external database or server.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
823 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (This project clearly illustrates how modern web technologies can be used to design real-time applications with minimal resources. The entire application logic, user authentication, vote processing, and result calculation are handled on the client side using JavaScript. This makes the system lightweight, easy to deploy, and suitable for small-scale elections such as classroom polls, college elections, and internal organizational voting. The automatic vote counting and real-time result generation features enhance transparency and eliminate errors associated with manual tallying processes.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
824 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 164 0 R
/E (In addition to its practical usefulness, the Polling Booth System also serves as a valuable learning platform for students. It provides hands-on experience in front-end web development, client-side scripting, data validation, and browser-based storage management. Students gain a deeper understanding of how interactive web applications are built and how data can be stored and retrieved using modern web APIs. The project also introduces fundamental concepts of software engineering such as requirement analysis, system design, implementation, testing, and evaluation.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
